# G064 — Family Tree Creator

*Pilot — real Innate syntax, v2. Prose insights from `family-tree.md`
still apply: self-referential DAG, transitive-closure invariant,
cost-budgeted BFS, set algebra over computed sets.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Notification of Mutual Connections

[mutual-connection @me @them
    IN ORDER TO
        notify @them that they share ancestors with @me
        but only if the relation is real and not vanishingly distant

    @my-ancestors    <- [people[ancestors {id: @me, max-depth: 4}]]
    @their-ancestors <- [people[ancestors {id: @them, max-depth: 4}]]
    @common          <- @my-ancestors ∩ @their-ancestors

    @common
        where
            size at least 1
            and size no more than 20
        IN ORDER TO
            avoid notifying on false positives (size 0 = unrelated)
            avoid notifying on "everyone descends from Charlemagne" noise
               (size > 20 = shared ancestor too distant to be meaningful)

    [draft
        to: @them
        body: [template[mutual-ancestor {common: @common:names}]]
    ]
    -> @draft
]

The choreography is natural because the domain concepts and the code
concepts are the same: *ancestors*, *common ancestors*, *related-ness
as a set-size threshold*. The resolver binds `∩` as set intersection
over entity lists.

---

## The Family Tree as a Named Choreography

A family tree is a self-referential graph. In Innate, the tree itself
is a named entity — the tree *is* the declaration.

[tree @root
    type: [[FamilyTree]]
    persons:  @people-set
    children: @children-index    ;; maintained reverse of parents
    next-id:  @next-person-id
]

[person @id
    type: [[Person]]
    name:    @name
    birth:   @birth?
    death:   @death?
    parents: @parent-ids          ;; 0, 1, or 2 @person references
]

When saved as `tree-smith.dpn`, other choreographies reference the
Smith family tree as `@tree-smith`. A `person` is always scoped to a
tree; `@tree-smith:@boughrest-ii` reads as "from the Smith tree, the
person named boughrest-ii."

---

## Verb Surface

[people
    new             {}                            -> @tree
    add             {tree, name, birth?, death?}  -> (@tree, @new-id)
    set-parents     {tree, child, parents}        -> @tree
        where
            no parent of @child may already be a descendant of @child
            or the commission resists with "would create a cycle"

    ancestors       {id, max-depth?}              -> [@person]
    descendants     {id, max-depth?}              -> [@person]
    common-ancestors {a, b}                       -> [@person]
    siblings        {id}                          -> [@person]
    cousins         {id, n}                       -> [@person]
]

The `set-parents` constraint is Innate's native idiom for what the v1
spec called a "transitive-closure invariant." The resolver checks the
constraint before committing; violation surfaces as resistance, which
the surrounding choreography can handle or escalate.

---

## Cycle prevention as resistance

The v1 model threw an exception on cycle; the Innate model emits
resistance that the caller can catch with fulfillment:

[genealogist-workflow @tree @child @proposed-parents
    IN ORDER TO record a claimed parent relationship safely

    [people[set-parents {tree: @tree, child: @child, parents: @proposed-parents}]]
        || [genealogist[resolve-conflict
                {tree: @tree, child: @child, proposed: @proposed-parents,
                 reason: "would create a cycle"}]]
]

If the edge would close a cycle, the resolver emits resistance
("would create a cycle"). The `||` routes to a human genealogist
who looks at the claim and decides whether the record is wrong
(some prior edge is mistaken) or the claim is wrong (the proposed
parent actually isn't one). This is exactly the shape of real
genealogy-database data-quality workflows.

---

## Bounded traversal is a `where` clause on distance

The v1 spec modeled `max_depth` as an optional parameter. In Innate,
the bound is naturally a `where` clause over the walk:

[people[ancestors {id: @me}
    where
        generation no more than 4 from @me
]

For a resolver that does raw BFS, this is equivalent to passing
`max-depth: 4`. For a resolver with precomputed ancestor sets, this
is a slice. The `where` clause is opaque to the surface syntax; the
resolver picks the strategy.

---

## Set algebra is first-class

The v1 spec used `@common <- @my-tree ∩ @their-tree` in the
choreographic case. The real Innate grammar doesn't define `∩` in the
base, but resolvers can register operators. A set-algebra resolver
provides:

- `@a ∩ @b` — intersection
- `@a ∪ @b` — union
- `@a - @b` — difference (elements in a not in b)
- `@a △ @b` — symmetric difference

This is the compositional layer over traversal that the v1 insight
called out. BFS gives the sets; set algebra composes them.

---

## Demo

[demo
    IN ORDER TO build a small three-generation tree and query it

    @t <- [people[new]]
    (@t, @alice) <- [people[add {tree: @t, name: "Alice", birth: 1930}]]
    (@t, @bob)   <- [people[add {tree: @t, name: "Bob", birth: 1932}]]
    (@t, @carol) <- [people[add {tree: @t, name: "Carol", birth: 1955}]]
    (@t, @dave)  <- [people[add {tree: @t, name: "Dave", birth: 1958}]]
    (@t, @eve)   <- [people[add {tree: @t, name: "Eve", birth: 1985}]]

    [people[set-parents {tree: @t, child: @carol, parents: [@alice, @bob]}]]
    [people[set-parents {tree: @t, child: @dave,  parents: [@alice, @bob]}]]
    [people[set-parents {tree: @t, child: @eve,   parents: [@carol]}]]

    -> [people[ancestors {id: @eve}]]
    ;; -> [@carol, @alice, @bob]

    -> [people[common-ancestors {a: @carol, b: @dave}]]
    ;; -> [@alice, @bob]     (siblings share both parents)

    -> [people[siblings {id: @carol}]]
    ;; -> [@dave]

    ;; Cycle prevention: try to make @alice a child of @eve (who is
    ;; @alice's great-grandchild through @carol). Resistance expected.
    [people[set-parents {tree: @t, child: @alice, parents: [@eve]}]]
        || -> "would create a cycle: @alice is already @eve's ancestor"
    ;; -> "would create a cycle: @alice is already @eve's ancestor"
]
